{"clock":1,"nonce":"5da1033bed47f29e81343e3b021f21cb","ops":[{"kind":"create","author":"61b55b322674a787b89ef011a06fca88d5ce49b7","time":1793973842,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/bimble","event":"issue:6244066675"},"data":{"body":"Pierre (6 Nov), with a screenshot of Revit's *Épaisseur des lignes* dialog:\n\n- **Model line weights**: 16 pen numbers; each has a thickness **per view scale** (columns 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:50, 1:100, 1:200, 1:500; columns can be added and removed). E.g. pen 1 = 0.10 mm at every scale; pen 7 = 2.0 mm at 1:5 … 0.5 mm at 1:500; pen 16 = 0.05–0.18 mm. They control the lines of objects (walls, columns, slabs…) in plans, sections and elevations, so a drawing's lines get thinner as its scale gets smaller.\n- **Perspective line weights**: 16 pens, one thickness each (3D / perspective views).\n- **Annotation line weights**: 16 pens, one thickness each, independent of scale (dimensions, text leaders, grids, tags).\n\nFor Bimble:\n- the graphic style gets a pen table (16 pens × scales for model lines; 16 for perspective; 16 for annotation); what is drawn refers to a **pen number** (cut outline, seen edges, lines above, hatches, frame, axes, dimensions…) instead of a width in mm, with the thickness looked up at the drawing's scale (between two listed scales: the nearest, as Revit does; to confirm);\n- an editor in the viewer like the screenshot (click a cell to change it; add/remove a scale);\n- defaults: Revit's metric table as in the screenshot, mapped from today's pens (cut 0.5, seen 0.18, above/hatch/annotation 0.13, frame 0.5) so current drawings don't change at 1:100;\n- on screen: thicker lines need a different line renderer (today edges are 1 pixel; style README notes it) — at least on paper first, then screen.\n\nAfter #34 (Pierre: \"do that next\").\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_","title":"Line weights: 16 pens per scale (model, perspective, annotation), like Revit"}},{"kind":"comment","author":"61b55b322674a787b89ef011a06fca88d5ce49b7","time":1793973932,"origin":{"forge":"github.com/guilbep/bimble","event":"comment:6686710230"},"data":{"body":"Pierre (6 Nov): the office's default **line types** (office prefix left out). Everything must be configurable (line types, pens, hatches).\n\nNamed line types = **colour × pattern × pen number**:\n- colours: black, red, grey, green, brown, orange, blue, pink, yellow;\n- patterns: *continu* (solid), *dash* (short dashes), *axes* (long dash – short dash, the grid-axis dash-dot of #23), *axes_detail* (finer dash-dot);\n- pen numbers 1, 2, 3, 5 (the pen table above gives the thickness at each scale).\nE.g. black_continu_1/2/3/5, black_dash_1/2/3/5, black_axes_1/2, black_axes_detail_1, red_*, grey_1_continu…, green_*, brown_*, orange_continu_1/2/3/5 and orange_dash_1/2/3, blue_1_continu…, pink_*.\n\nFor Bimble: a configurable list of line types (name, colour, dash pattern, pen number) in the graphic style, these as defaults; what is drawn (cut outlines, seen edges, above, axes, dimensions, frame) refers to a line type by name.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_"}}]}